Z||000002||Formula||10-28-1999||07:23 PM||||anyone think about the lumina z34 3.4 twin dual (yes 4) cam motor. It is fwd and 220 HP.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Eddie||10-28-1999||08:40 PM||||The z34 has 210 hp my wife has a 1993 its mint. I always eye it up .I wish a tree would fall on it some times so my fiero could have the motor. but its fun to drive it kicks!||||reg||8
Z||000004||Shiner||10-28-1999||10:04 PM||||Actually, I belive the 3.4 TDC is actually a DUAL cam engine.  ||||reg||5
Z||000005||SCCA FIERO||10-28-1999||10:48 PM||||TDC = Twin Dual Cam (4 total)||||reg||6
Z||000006||Tim||10-28-1999||10:58 PM||||Twin, two heads<BR>Dual Cams per head. One for exhaust and one for intake. cam belts and idler pulley high wear problem.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Eddie||10-29-1999||07:15 PM||||THATS RIGHT 4 CAMS 24 VALVE <BR>		EDDIE<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000008||Tim||10-29-1999||09:18 PM||||The 3.4 crate engine is rated 160hp with carb and single exhaust! I understand crate engine has more cam than f-body 3.4 The crate engine is cheaper than re-building a 2.8 or 3.1. For starter holes use the 2.8 or any FWD small v-6 to make a template, the oil pan holes are the reference points. Ream out bell housing bolt hole on the axle side and grind the ear of the oil pan rail. Use the 2.8 as a guide. <P>Installed 3.4 crate last year.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||loafer87gt||01-20-2000||07:16 PM||||Is this true that the 160 HP are for a cabuerated car? The numbers should be a lot higher if swapped in a Fiero, should they not? Also, if the crate engine is setup for a carb, what do you have to change to get it to work with our fuel injection?||||reg||1
Z||000010||WKDFIRO||01-20-2000||07:46 PM||||Whatever the numbers, the 3.4 does give a substantial kick in the pants over the 2.8. The question about getting fuel to the cylinders wheather it be carb or fuel injected is academic. The great thing about the 3.4 is that the 2.8 intake plenems, the exhaust, the distributer just about everything from a Fiero can be put on the long block. Along with the plenums comes your fuel injectors. For us in Calif. it is great to get more power without having to worry about getting past the smog. It will smog and as long as you don't confuse the guy by telling him what it is he wont know.<BR>As for the 3.4 Twin Cam, Chris West has one with a turbo charger installed. He was gracious enough to allow me to drive it for a lap at an SCCA Solo practice session and I can tell you that it was something. He told me I babied it and should have redlined it at 7 grand. I would not have felt comfortable driving a car that is not mine to an rpm line that is off the tach dial! It was great though! You can honestly forget about the back cylinder plugs the engine truly takes up the ENTIRE engine bay.<P>Besides it not the increase from 140 hp to 160, it the improvement in torque from 165 to 200 lb/ft of torque. What this means is that the need to down shift from 5 th to 4th is almost unnecessary. Gotta go? GO!<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 01-20-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000011||cobra427||01-20-2000||08:28 PM||||I would just like to add that installing all the 2.8 parts to the 3.4 long block(upper,middle,lower plenum,fuelrail etc.)really makes for a nice package.  Take note tho that the 3.4 injectors are recommended for this swap.   Makes sense to me.||||reg||1
Z||000012||loafer87gt||01-20-2000||08:46 PM||||This sounds like it might be an allright route to go. The only problem is the starter location. How long would it take a shop to do the relocation? Also, I was told by GM that the warranty is void on the engine because the vehicle I'm putting it in isn't the designed application (mean for RWD s-10 truck) and that modifying the block also voids it. This is the only thing that really bothers me about the swap. Each option seems to have its merits, if I go for the 2.8 longblock, I get a 2 year warranty, if I spend $300 more for 3.4 crate, I get a performance boost but no protection or warranty. Its a tough decision to make.||||reg||1
Z||000013||fieroshop||01-20-2000||08:49 PM||||I'm doing a 3.4 conversion as we speak and it will require the 3.4 injectors and an adjustable fuel pressure regulator. Also per Ed Parks the 3.4 is under rated and it is more like 180 hp. I am having a template made so that I can do more 3.4 conversions at our shop. The interesting swap will be a 3.1 turbo out of a 90 Grand Prix.||||reg||7
Z||000014||cobra427||01-20-2000||09:03 PM||||fieroshop, do you really have a genuine 90's G/P turbo? They're kind of rare aren't they? Maybe you should think about doing a resto on that and do a separate 3.1 tubo in a Fiero.<BR>they only made thos in 89/90. Just a thought.||||reg||1
Z||000015||william_thorniii||01-20-2000||09:11 PM||||There are several members of the fiero-list that have done or have said that they have done this swap.  I really don't understand it myself.  Tom, why go to all of the work for 205 bhp?  Although the bypass valve can be dialed in to allow for more boost from the stock 5 and that would probably get you near the 210-220 range.  It seems like a TDC would be a better alternative.  They are more readily available, less expensive, more powerful, better engine, more reliable... need I go on?  One of my friends has a 89 GPT.  In stock form, it is a rather unimpressive vehicle in the performance category.  The seats on the other hand are awesome!<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000016||WKDFIRO||01-21-2000||01:03 AM||||Of all the 3.4s that have come out of Fiero Motorsports (going past 60 now), NONE of them have needed regulators. None have had problems with fuel delivery...why would they need them if they are using the same fuel injector rails from the 3.4???<P>BTW - Those Grand Prix Turbos were SOOO cool!!<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 01-21-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||DISenchanted||01-21-2000||02:50 AM||||WKDFIRO:<P>Am I correct in assuming that you work for Fiero Motorsports?  If so, what dis the average price for a 3.4 conversion?  I'm about 20 miles from there and I've been thinking about it.  As soon as I get my next fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Alex4mula||01-21-2000||09:21 AM||||I'm interested in a 3.4 crate instead of a 3.2 remanufactured but I am afraid that adding the 3.4 injectors cost would bring it a lot higher than the 3.2. Any one knows how much $$ will the 3.4 injectors will cost me? I recently blue printed mines (Marren Motorsports). ||||reg||1
Z||000019||Raydar||01-21-2000||09:56 AM||||On Ed Parks page, he says that you can also use the injectors from a Chevy smallblock TPI engine (I think a 5.0) Might be cheaper. Just a suggestion.||||reg||1
Z||000020||mwbackus||01-21-2000||08:05 PM||||Sorry to have to use a four letter word here on the forum, but the 19lb/hr FORD Motorsports injectors are of the same design (multec) as those used on the Fiero MPFI.  They have the correct resistance, and cost less than $200 for a set of 8. They are currently available from Summit:<P>FMS-M9593C302   19 lb/hr flow    kit $193.95 <P>If four interested parties were to consolidate orders and buy 3 sets, the resulting cost for a set of 6 NEW injectors would be less than $150.  Since I have plans for a 3.4 project in the near future, I would be a willing candidate for a group purchase such as this.<P><BR>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000021||HF6582||01-22-2000||06:12 AM||||I have a set of 19# injectors out of my 89 mustang that if any one wants them you can have them just pay shipping.They have 92k on them and you will need new o-rings, worked when removed put bigger motor and injectors in.<BR>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000022||HF6582||01-22-2000||06:13 AM||||I have a set of 19# injectors out of my 89 mustang that if any one wants them you can have them just pay shipping.They have 92k on them and you will need new o-rings, worked when removed put bigger motor and injectors in.<BR>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000023||batboy||01-22-2000||12:15 PM||||Thorniii, I have to respectfully disagree with some of your previous statement:<P>"It seems like a TDC would be a better alternative. They are more readily available, less expensive, more powerful, better engine, more reliable..."<P>In my opinion they are less reliable than the standard pushrod/single cam engine and are high maintenance. Around here, they're very hard to find because so many have shot craps and had to be replaced. The timing belts go out all the time, usually destroying pistons and valves when it does. The valvetrain is ultra expensive too. More powerful maybe, at least more HP, I don't know what the TQ is, but usually with high reving engines, there is a lack of low end torque. Most of my street driving is in the 2000-4000 rpm range, not 5000-7000. Now if this was for racing, then that would be a different story. Like I said, this is just my opinion and personal observations.||||reg||1
Z||000024||la9||01-22-2000||12:30 PM||||I have to agree with Batboy. I thought the 3.4 was a good idea but I didn't realize it was TDC. Doesn't sound like much fun changing the timing belt in a fiero compartment and the belt changing intervals creep up faster than you think.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000025||olympic||01-22-2000||01:40 PM||||la9,<BR>   There are 2 different engines that are 3.4l in displacement. One is a 24 valve DOHC (or TDC as some call it) which came in various GM FWD cars.  The other is a pushrod 12 valve engine used in rear wheel drive applications like the camaro and S-10.  It is the pushrod version that is quite similar to the Fiero 2.8.  It is the one that requires moving the starter to the other side.||||reg||1
Z||000026||batboy||01-22-2000||04:46 PM||||Yes, I guess I didn't make myself clear on that point. I was comparing the 3.4 single cam engine (which is a good motor) to the 3.4 engine with 4 overhead cams. The TDC 3.4 is more expensive and more work to swap in. It requires an ECM and wiring harness swap too. You think those firewall side spark plugs are hard to change now? Maybe you ought to look at a TDC 3.4 that's sitting in a Fiero. I have more room in the engine compartment with my V8 Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000027||terryk||01-23-2000||12:32 AM||||Does someone have first hand knowledge of a DOHC (3.4L TDC) that had the valves touch the pistons when the cam belt broke? They are non-interference motors.<P>No FOAF's allow!<P>I've seen one installed in a Fiero (in detail) and the worst thing was the rear plugs are difficult to change. The cradle needs to be dropped about 3 inches to do it.<BR>Yes, some hoses need to be fabricated for the cooling and heater.<P>The DOHC's aren't as bad as people make out. Certainly, you want to change the tensioners BEFORE you install it.||||reg||1
Z||000028||batboy||01-23-2000||02:40 AM||||I've had several mechanics, including my high school buddy who owns his own repair shop, tell me that. A guy I work with bought a used Monte Carlo a while back with a 3.1, but he was looking at a TDC 3.4 one first. The saleman, who he bought cars from before, told him not to buy the TDC 3.4 because they had so many problems with it. All American Auto Salvage in Topeka told me that they rarely have those engines in stock because so many go bad and get replaced. What's FOAF mean?<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 01-23-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000029||terryk||01-23-2000||10:18 AM||||Batboy,<P>Yeah, I agree that the DOHC's are somewhat rare in the yards. Around here (No. Cal.), they are being bought up for engine swaps. The only issue I've heard the mechanics talk about is the tensioner/belt failures but the updates are available. Expensive engines though. No bottom end, but killer top end.<P>"Friend Of A Friend" FOAF. <p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 01-23-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000030||batboy||01-23-2000||09:40 PM||||For some guys out there, this TDC 3.4 might be ideal, like screaming up a canyon highway or open road racing. I've heard the same thing about those belts having to be replaced often. You really have to keep up with the maintenance. What I'm afraid of though, is that a regular Joe Cool will say, wow look at all that horsepower, then spend all that money and time doing the swap, only to go out Saturday night cruising and be majorly disappointed to find out it lacks low end torque and can be temperamental like any other exotic engine.||||reg||1
Z||000031||WKDFIRO||01-23-2000||10:40 PM||||DIS,<P>   I work at Fiero Motorsports when I get time off from my other job essentially. Everything I learned about Fieros I learned from Keith "Fiero Doc" Huff and John Parenti. I seldom have seen 3.4s go in without extras. Pushing the price up to around $2000 grand. (headers, perf. heads, perf clutch etc.) so I will tell you what I tell everybody. Give em a call. Tell em what you want and they will do it. They will also tell you what they have learned and let you know if you're wasting your money. The great thing about FM is that all of their business is by word of mouth. No advertising save for their phone number and address in the Fiero Owner (FOCOA). Take the time to go down there and take a look, chances are that they have a Fiero in the air getting a 3.4. When I don't work there I usually just hang out there.<P>As a matter of fact, if I can shamelessly promote my club, Fieros West meets monthly at Fiero Motorsports the 1st Saturday of every month. Starts around 2 pm. If you really want to get to know about your Fiero there is usually a lot of people who would love to share their experiences with you on what works for them and what didnt. <P>Doh, forgot their number, here it is: 714 639-9005<P>Okay, Im done now, sorry if that got long.<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 01-23-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000032||WKDFIRO||01-23-2000||10:45 PM||||And actually there are 3 3.4 V6 engine configurations. The DOHC (aka TDC which means Top Dead Center to me but oh well...)the FWD with aluminum heads and the 3.4 RWD used in the S-10, and the F-bodies in the mid 90s. All are VERY different. As I understand it the RWD is the one that fits the easiest into a Fiero. I still don't know what you guys are talking about sawing off a part of the block to clear something or other....||||reg||2
Z||000033||terryk||01-23-2000||10:50 PM||||batboy,<P>I agree completely. The DOHC is great for passing and screaming but only average for street drags. Any NA Buick in a Fiero would take it off the line.<P>I have a couple of Diacom runs where a guy  hit 7650 RPM. He took it to 8000 once. It sounded gooooooood! I drove it and noticed that it basically felt like a strong Fiero 3.1L, but pulled even better from 4000-7000.<P>On the 3.4L block conversion, check:<BR> <A HREF="http://members.home.net/sprigint/x11p/engine/engine.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.home.net/sprigint/x11p/engine/engine.html</A>  <P>for great details and photos.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 01-23-2000).]||||reg||1
